<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Salvadoran Civil War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1979">1979</a>–<a href="/years/1992">1992</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Salvadoran Civil War was fought from 1979 to 1992 between the military-led government of El <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salvador">Salvador</a>, backed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/farabundo-marti-national-liberation-front">Farabundo Martí National Liberation Front</a>, a coalition of left-wing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> groups. More than 75,000 people died, many in massacres of civilians such as El Mozote. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chapultepec-peace-accords">Chapultepec Peace Accords</a> of 1992 ended the war and turned the FMLN into a political party.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/salvadoran-civil-war.jpg" alt="Salvadoran Civil War" width="320" height="294" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Images compiled by PizzaKing13 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Salvadoran_Civil_War_image_collage.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>El <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salvador">Salvador</a> has long been marked by extreme inequality. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">Coffee</a> became its main cash crop in the late 19th century, and the divide between rich and poor widened through the 1920s and after the fall in coffee prices that followed the crash of 1929. In 1932 the Central American Socialist Party led an uprising of peasants and Indigenous people, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-matanza">La Matanza</a> the armed forces killed approximately 30,000 civilians. Military <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dictatorship">dictatorships</a> then monopolised political power while protecting the landowning elite, and opposition grew among middle-class, working-class and poor Salvadorans through the 20th century. The later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> coalition took its name from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/farabundo-marti">Farabundo Martí</a>, a leader of the 1932 uprising.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>On 15 October 1979 a civil-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/military-junta">military junta</a> deposed President Romero in a coup, and killings of anti-coup protesters by the government are widely seen as the start of the war. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> feared a communist revolution like those in Nicaragua and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> and backed the new government, with economic aid and with training and equipment for the army; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/farabundo-marti-national-liberation-front">FMLN</a> was backed by Cuba, Nicaragua and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>. On 24 March 1980 Archbishop <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oscar-romero">Óscar Romero</a>, who had written to President Carter asking him to suspend military aid, was assassinated while celebrating Mass. On 2 December 1980 members of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-guard-panama">National Guard</a> were suspected of raping and murdering four American church women.</p><p>The army adopted a scorched-earth strategy of draining the sea, aimed at the civilian population in the countryside. In March 1981 troops swept through Cabañas Department, and the air force bombed and strafed fleeing civilians at the Río Lempa. On 11 December 1981 the Atlácatl Battalion massacred at least 733, possibly up to 1,000, unarmed civilians at El Mozote, including 146 children. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-napoleon-duarte">José Napoleón Duarte</a> won the 1984 presidential election, held under military rule, with 54 per cent of the vote. On 11 November 1989 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/farabundo-marti-national-liberation-front">FMLN</a> launched its final offensive, bringing the fighting into the wealthy suburbs of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-salvador">San Salvador</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chapultepec-peace-accords">Chapultepec Peace Accords</a> were signed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city">Mexico City</a> on 16 January 1992. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> reports that the war killed more than 75,000 people and left about 8,000 disappeared, and it estimated that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/farabundo-marti-national-liberation-front">FMLN</a> was responsible for 5 per cent of atrocities and the security forces for 85 per cent. The accords reduced the army from a wartime high of 63,000 to 32,000 by 1993, abolished the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-guard-panama">National Guard</a> and Treasury Police, and created a National Civil Police, which deployed its first officers in March 1993. A 1993 amnesty law hindered accountability until the Supreme Court of Justice ruled it unconstitutional in 2016.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Salvadoran Civil War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
